DRMINFO(1)                       User Commands                      DRMINFO(1)

NAME
       drminfo - report the display-manager device and what it can do

SYNOPSIS
       drminfo [-n node] [-v]

DESCRIPTION
       drminfo  opens each node under /dev/dri, asks it which driver is behind
       it, what capabilities that driver advertises and which mode-setting ob-
       jects it exposes, and prints the lot.  Where the driver  is  vmwgfx  it
       also asks for the device parameters that decide what the graphics stack
       above  can  do  --  whether  the device has 3D at all, its shader-model
       level, how much memory it will accept for surfaces and buffers.

       It reads all of this through the  kernel's  own  interface  with  plain
       ioctl(2)  calls and no library in between.  That is the point: it still
       answers when the userspace graphics stack is broken or  missing,  which
       is  exactly  when  the  question  "did  the kernel find a GPU" is being
       asked.   The  tools  that  use  libdrm  --  modetest(1),   proptest(1),
       vbltest(1) -- go deeper but need that stack to be working.

       With  no  arguments  every  node  is examined.  Opening a node needs no
       privilege beyond the permissions on it: card0 is readable by the  video
       group, renderD128 by anyone.

OPTIONS
       -n node
              Examine  one node.  The argument is either a path or a bare num-
              ber, in which case /dev/dri/cardN is used.

       -v     Print every mode of every connector, rather than the first few.

OUTPUT
       For each node:

       driver The driver's name and its interface version --  on  this  system
              vmwgfx  2.20.0  --  followed  by its date and description.  This
              version is a contract: it is what Mesa's svga driver checks  be-
              fore it will use the device.

       capabilities
              The  generic  capabilities,  one per line.  The interesting ones
              are DUMB_BUFFER  (the  device  can  allocate  a  plain  scan-out
              buffer),  PRIME  (buffers can be passed between processes as de-
              scriptors, which is what lets the browser hand  rendered  frames
              to the compositor without copying them), and TIMESTAMP_MONOTONIC
              (vertical-blank events carry a monotonic timestamp).

       vmwgfx parameters
              Present only for that driver.  3D is 1 when the device offers 3D
              acceleration  at  all; where it is 0 -- some hypervisors present
              the device without it -- Mesa falls back to  its  software  ras-
              teriser  and  everything  still works, more slowly.  SM4_1 , SM5
              and GL43 say how far the shader model goes, which  is  what  de-
              cides the OpenGL version Mesa can advertise.

       mode setting
              The  counts of CRTCs, connectors, encoders and framebuffers, the
              range of framebuffer sizes the device will scan  out,  and  then
              each object: connectors with their type, connection state, phys-
              ical  size  and  modes;  encoders with the CRTCs they can drive;
              CRTCs with the mode currently set on them; planes with the pixel
              formats they accept.

EXIT STATUS
       0      At least one node was examined.

       1      There is no /dev/dri -- no GPU driver bound to any device --  or
              the named node could not be opened.  This is the normal state on
              a  machine  whose  display device this kernel has no driver for;
              the console runs on the framebuffer and the X server falls  back
              to /etc/X11/xorg.conf and software rendering.

       2      A bad argument.

SEE ALSO
       modetest(1), proptest(1), vbltest(1), Xorg(1)

